task: dataflow-seek
agent: opus-dataflow
started: 2026-09-12
branch: feature/dataflow-seek
worktree: ../okay-wt-dsk1
spec: specs/dataflow.md, stage 11 box 2

why: a resumed run replays every partition from offset zero. With the
  log as the source (box 1) that is the one cost a log exists to
  remove: the position is a number, and a session could open AT it.

what: positions on the wire and a seek on resume — for the sinks that
  can. The design the last session wrote predicted "one Long and one
  signature", and reading the code says it was wrong, which is the
  first thing this lane records.

  A WINDOWED operator keeps its open panes INSIDE the partition and
  hands them over only when they close. A fresh session opened at
  epoch N-1's position has none of them, and their contributions
  from before that position reach nobody: seeking is UNSOUND for a
  windowed sink without either delta handovers every epoch (a change
  to okay.Windows and to the merge traffic) or a replay bounded by
  the window horizon. Both are real designs; neither is this lane.

  fold and keyed sinks already hand over DELTAS and clear — `peek`
  empties the map — so for them a fresh session at the position is
  exactly right, and that is what lands:

  - `Flow.Src` thunks take a start (elements to skip); `Flow.slices`
    seeks natively, `Flow.of` drops (which IS the replay, named)
  - `Sink.seekable`: fold/keyed true, windowed false, `and` both
  - `Req.Open` carries (from, epoch); `Resp.Epoch` carries `consumed`;
    the journal carries positions per partition
  - on resume — and on a replacement worker MID-RUN, which is the
    same road — a seekable sink's sessions open at the journalled
    position and epoch; a windowed sink's open at zero, as today

  Tests: a keyed job over the log resumed after a coordinator death
  reads FEWER records than the topic holds (a counting Topic says so)
  and answers the batch answer; a windowed job on the same schedule
  reads at least the whole topic again, and also answers it.

predict: keyed seeks and windowed replays, both asserted by count.
  The design for windowed seeking — box 2b — goes in the spec with the
  two roads and their costs, not in the code.
